\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The positive gauge vacuum}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{For the \vocabk{pure-gauge transfer operator}{fdip-007Z}
 on #{\mathcal H=L^2(X,\nu)}, where #{\nu} is product Haar
 probability, the leading eigenvalue
 #{\lambda_0=\|T\|>0} is simple. Its positive normalized
 eigenvector #{\Omega} has an everywhere positive Borel
 representative, bounded above and below by positive constants,
 satisfying
##{T\Omega(U)=\lambda_0\Omega(U)\qquad(U\in X).}
This representative is pointwise gauge invariant. If the spatial
 potential #{V} is continuous, #{\Omega} is continuous.
 Consequently
##{\Omega\in\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}},\qquad
 \|T_{\mathrm{phys}}\|=\|T\|.}
The vector #{\Omega} specifies the [vacuum state](fdip-00H8)
 #{A\mapsto\langle\Omega,A\Omega\rangle} on bounded physical
 operators.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{K_T(U,U')=b(U)c(U,U')b(U')}. The bounds on
 #{b,b^{-1}} and strict positivity of the continuous kernel #{c}
 give constants #{0<m\le K_T\le M<\infty}. By
 \ref{fdip-0080}, #{T} is a nonzero positive compact
 self-adjoint operator, so its norm is a positive eigenvalue.
 For a normalized top eigenvector #{u}, kernel positivity gives
##{\lambda_0=\langle u,Tu\rangle
 \le\langle |u|,T|u|\rangle\le\lambda_0.}
Equality in the Rayleigh bound implies #{T|u|=\lambda_0|u|}:
 the positive operator #{\lambda_0\bbI -T} has zero quadratic form
 there, hence its square root annihilates #{|u|}.
 Replacing #{|u|} by #{\lambda_0^{-1}T|u|} gives a strictly
 positive representative. Normalize it as #{\Omega}.}

\p{If a real top eigenvector #{v} were nonzero and orthogonal
 to #{\Omega}, its positive and negative sets would both have
 positive measure. On their product,
 #{|v(U)||v(U')|-v(U)v(U')>0}; elsewhere this quantity is
 nonnegative. The lower bound on #{K_T} therefore gives
##{\langle |v|,T|v|\rangle>\langle v,Tv\rangle
 =\lambda_0\|v\|^2,}
contradicting the norm bound. Since the kernel is real, real
 and imaginary parts of a complex top eigenvector are real top
 eigenvectors. Removing its component along #{\Omega} and using
 the preceding argument proves simplicity over #{\mathbb C}.}

\p{The continuum comparison of \ref{fdip-0080} gives
 #{TR_h=R_hT}, so #{R_h\Omega} is a normalized top eigenvector.
 The representative of #{\Omega} is positive at every point, and
 the gauge action reindexes that argument, so the translate is
 positive as well. Simplicity forces
 #{R_h\Omega=\Omega} as an #{L^2} class. Thus the full top
 eigenvector is physical; the restriction norm is at least
 #{\lambda_0} and at most #{\|T\|}, proving equality.}

\p{For the promised representative, use
##{\Omega(U)=\lambda_0^{-1}b(U)
 \int_X c(U,U')b(U')\Omega(U')\,\extd\nu(U').}
The integral is continuous in #{U}: #{c} is uniformly
 continuous on the compact product and #{b\Omega\in L^1}.
 It is bounded above and below by positive multiples of
 #{\int b\Omega\,\extd\nu>0}. Multiplication by bounded positive
 Borel #{b} gives the stated Borel bounds. This representative
 equals the eigenvector almost everywhere. Substituting it into
 the integral leaves the integral unchanged, proving the
 eigen-equation at every #{U}. Simultaneous gauge covariance of
 #{K_T}, invariance of Haar measure and invariance of the
 eigenvector class give #{\Omega(h\cdot U)=\Omega(U)} at
 every point. If #{b} is continuous, the displayed formula
 is continuous as well.}
}

\p{The compact spectral premise is the one used in
 \ref{fdip-0085}; strict kernel positivity supplies the additional
 simplicity and representative assertions proved here.}
